<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanie/">Aylanie</a></td><td>27</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Eylani/">Eylani</a></td><td>21</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailanna/">Ailanna</a></td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanis/">Aylanis</a></td><td>20</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailanis/">Ailanis</a></td><td>17</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanna/">Aylanna</a></td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailannys/">Ailannys</a></td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylannie/">Aylannie</a></td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailanee/">Ailanee</a></td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailanii/">Ailanii</a></td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanee/">Aylanee</a></td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Ailannie/">Ailannie</a></td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanii/">Aylanii</a></td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td><a href="/name/Aylanny/">Aylanny</a></td><td>5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The boys'' side is smaller but shaped the same way. <a href="/name/Eithan/">Eithan</a>, <a href="/name/Neithan/">Neithan</a>, and <a href="/name/Eitham/">Eitham</a> added up to 4,033 births in 2025. All three are respellings of Ethan. All three climb the SSA charts as if they were three different names.</p>
<h2>Nobody agrees on what it means, because nobody chose it for what it means</h2>
<p>Baby-name sites cannot settle on an origin for Ailany. Spanish, meaning "holy." Hebrew, by way of Eliana, meaning "God has answered." Hawaiian, by way of Ailani, meaning "chief" or "island." Three unrelated language families, three unrelated meanings, one name. That is not a name with a contested etymology. It is a name that was never chosen for its etymology at all. It was chosen for how it sounds, and the meaning got backfilled afterward by whoever wrote the baby-name-site entry.</p>
<h2>Where it''s happening</h2>
<p>Eithan and Ailany over-index by almost exactly the same amount in the same two states: Texas and California, both around double their expected share. Public name-and-ethnicity data puts Eithan at 52% Hispanic, the largest single group by a wide margin. Ailani, the older root name, shows the same pattern one state over: concentrated in California and Arizona rather than Texas, consistent with a name that took hold in Southwestern Hispanic naming culture years earlier and is only now producing a national wave of copies.</p>
<h2>The receipt</h2>
<p>Eithan and Neithan are two different spellings, filed as two different names, entered by two different sets of parents who very likely never spoke to each other. Their year-over-year growth:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Eithan</th><th>Neithan</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>2021</td><td>280</td><td>30</td></tr>
<tr><td>2022</td><td>605</td><td>43</td></tr>
<tr><td>2023</td><td>876</td><td>67</td></tr>
<tr><td>2024</td><td>1,599</td><td>137</td></tr>
<tr><td>2025</td><td>3,636</td><td>371</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Neither grew at a steady pace. Eithan''s worst year was +45%, Neithan''s best was +171%. But over the full four years, Eithan multiplied by about 13x and Neithan by about 12x, landing within 5% of each other despite never moving in the same increments in the same year. That is not two names having good years on their own schedules. That is one sound moving through the country, arriving on birth certificates spelled a dozen different ways, exactly the way <a href="/blog/how-america-stopped-sharing-names/">How America Stopped Sharing Names</a> said it would.</p>', 'published', 'NobodyNamed', '/api/og/default', '2026-09-21T09:00:00.000Z', datetime('now'))
